{
  "id": "Compound::DB00294 - Gene::6462",
  "verbalization": "Etonogestrel is implicated in the binding of Shbg through a shared pathway involving Ethinyl Estradiol, a known therapeutic steroid hormone. The evidence suggests that Etonogestrel may cause clinical manifestations such as coital bleeding, vaginal contraceptive device expulsion, or foreign body formation, all of which are linked to Ethinyl Estradiol. Ethinyl Estradiol, in turn, binds to Shbg, indicating a potential mechanism by which Etonogestrel could influence Shbg levels. The lowest common ancestor of these relationships is the therapeutic steroid hormone class, suggesting a common biological function. The integration of these paths supports the hypothesis that Etonogestrel may modulate Shbg through the action of Ethinyl Estradiol, though the exact nature of this modulation remains to be elucidated. The evidence is consistent with a mechanistic link, but further experimental validation is required to confirm the direct interaction between Etonogestrel and Shbg.",
